dict(n=3, orig=4, answer='E', type='Main conclusion',
     passage=[('ctx', 'Adults over the age of 60 are often depicted by the media as depressed, lonely and grumpy. Of course, old age is associated with physical decline, health issues become more common and memory gets fuzzier.'),
              ('counter', 'Previously, theories of ageing assumed that emotional experiences would follow a similar pathway towards dysfunction.'),
              ('conc', 'They were wrong.'),
              ('evid', 'Recent research has shown that in old age negative emotions (like anger) wane, while positive emotions (like contentment) become more common.'),
              ('prem', 'Why? Our lives undergo pronounced changes in later life. Work demands are eliminated, there is more time for leisure pursuits, and older people structure their environments in ways that reduce unnecessary stress. Time horizons shrink: people learn to spend their remaining precious time on the things that matter most.')],
     stem='Which one of the following best expresses the main conclusion of the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'Not everyone experiences the negative physical decline in old age often depicted in the media.',
              'B': 'In old age people experience both positive and negative emotions less strongly.',
              'C': 'Being freed from the demands of work allows retired people to feel happier.',
              'D': 'Older people find better ways of avoiding stress and using their time productively.',
              'E': 'Assumptions that the emotional aspects of ageing mirror the physical aspects were wrong.'},
     right='“They were wrong” is the verdict on the old theories that emotions follow physical decline. E spells it out in full.',
     why={'A': 'The passage accepts physical decline. Its point is about emotions.',
          'B': 'Contradicts the research: positive emotions become <b>more</b> common.',
          'C': 'One part of the explanation (“Why?”), not the conclusion.',
          'D': 'Also part of the explanation, and “productively” is never said.'},
     gap='A short sentence like “They were wrong” can be the conclusion. Rewrite it in full: who was wrong, about what?'),

dict(n=4, orig=5, answer='B', type='Parallel reasoning',
     passage=[('prem', 'You are allowed to vote in the general election only if you are at least 18 years old.'),
              ('prem', 'You have reached 18'),
              ('conc', 'so you will be able to vote in the forthcoming election.')],
     stem='Which one of the following most closely parallels the reasoning used in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'You cannot obtain foreign currency from this bank if you cannot produce acceptable identification. You cannot produce the required identification, therefore we cannot let you have the foreign currency.',
              'B': 'You can visit your grandmother in hospital providing you have no illness yourself. As you have no illness, you will be able to visit your grandmother.',
              'C': 'We can afford to go to America this year only if you save up sufficient money. You haven’t saved enough so we will not be able to go.',
              'D': 'To win the jackpot prize in the national lottery you need a ticket with all six prize-winning numbers. When I heard you had won the jackpot, I knew you had the lucky ticket that matched all the numbers from the draw.',
              'E': 'Passport applications can only be accepted if photographs are witnessed as a likeness by someone in a professional job. Your application was refused so your witness cannot have been a professional person.'},
     right='Same shape: a condition for being allowed to do something is met, so you will be able to do it.',
     why={'A': 'The condition is <b>not</b> met, so no currency: the opposite direction.',
          'C': 'Condition not met (not enough saved), so no trip. Denies the condition.',
          'D': 'Works back from the result (won) to the condition (six numbers).',
          'E': 'Works back from the result (refused) to the condition (not a professional).'},
     working_title='Working: strip it to a skeleton',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Original: you can do <b>B</b> (vote) only if <b>A</b> (18 or over) {ar('pen')} you have A {ar('pen')} so you <b>will</b> get B.</p>
<p>The flaw: A is needed but may not be enough (you might not be registered).</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>B</b><span>Can do B (visit) providing A (no illness) → you have A → so you will get B. {tick()} Same shape.</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>No A (ID) → no B (currency). You don’t have A → so no B. Condition failed. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>B (America) only if A (savings) → no A → so no B. Condition failed. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>Need A (six numbers) for B (jackpot) → B happened → so you had A. Starts from the result. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>B (accepted) only if A (professional witness) → not B → so not A. Starts from the result. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Check which part the middle sentence gives you: the condition (A) or the result (B), and whether it is met or not.', 'tip')}"""),

dict(n=5, orig=9, answer='C', type='Draw a conclusion',
     passage=[('ctx', 'Microscopic malaria parasites enter a person’s bloodstream through mosquito bites and infect red blood cells. They replicate in these cells and penetrate other organs.'),
              ('ctx', 'Until recently, microbiologists sought to block the parasites from entering red blood cells.'),
              ('evid', 'Rhode Island University studied 700 Tanzanian children infected with malaria and found the parasites use a protein, since named PfSEA-1, to escape from the red blood cells they infect.'),
              ('evid', 'Having investigated a subgroup of children immune to malaria, the researchers realised these children were producing an antibody that locked protein PfSEA-1 into their red blood cells. Trapped in the red cells, the parasites were destroyed by immune system cells.'),
              ('evid', 'Scientists have reproduced the antibody and successfully tested it on mice. Experts believe that, after trials, the antibody will be certified for use in the treatment of humans.')],
     stem='Which one of the following can be drawn as a conclusion from the above passage?',
     options={'A': 'Rhode Island University is a world leader in medical research.',
              'B': 'After the discovery of protein PfSEA-1, the traditional approaches to fight malaria will be abandoned as ineffective.',
              'C': 'The discovery of protein PfSEA-1 is likely to help scientists to develop a new way of combatting malaria.',
              'D': 'World leaders should support and fund the research being conducted by Rhode Island University.',
              'E': 'A unique feature of the Tanzanian children helped in the discovery of protein PfSEA-1 and its function in spreading malaria.'},
     right='Old approach: block entry. New idea: trap the parasite inside the cell, already working in mice and expected to be used in humans. So PfSEA-1 is likely to lead to a new treatment.',
     why={'A': 'One study doesn’t make the university a world leader.',
          'B': 'Too strong: nothing says the old approaches don’t work or will be dropped.',
          'D': 'A recommendation about funding that the passage never makes.',
          'E': 'Nothing says the immunity is unique to Tanzanian children, and the protein helps parasites escape cells, not spread malaria.'},
     gap='A drawn conclusion stays as cautious as the evidence: “likely to help” matches “experts believe”.'),

dict(n=6, orig=10, answer='C', type='Weaken',
     passage=[('ctx', 'Despite the government’s promise to be more business friendly, plans have been announced to change the law regarding the paid leave that couples are entitled to take following the birth of a child. This will allow new parents to take more time off work than under the current regulations.'),
              ('counter', 'It is claimed that the current legislation makes it difficult for those with family commitments to manage all of their responsibilities,'),
              ('prem', 'but it needs to be recognised that businesses (and in particular small businesses) need to operate in a way that guarantees that staff are available to do the jobs that need doing. The new proposals are undoubtedly going to make the system more complicated and could dissuade businesses from employing certain groups of people.'),
              ('conc', 'They should be opposed.')],
     stem='Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'The example given is only one of a range of measures being proposed.',
              'B': 'Many businesses now allow their employees to choose to work from home.',
              'C': 'The proposals from the government provide support for businesses to help with the cost of covering the work of staff taking leave.',
              'D': 'The problems associated with covering the work of absent members of staff become increasingly difficult the longer the absence goes on.',
              'E': 'The proposals will increase the amount of leave that employees are allowed to take in other circumstances as well as following the birth of a child.'},
     right='The worry is that businesses can’t cover absent staff, so they may avoid hiring some groups. Help with the cost of cover takes away much of that worry.',
     why={'A': 'We aren’t told what the other measures do, so this changes nothing.',
          'B': 'Working from home doesn’t help when someone is on leave and not working at all.',
          'D': 'Longer absences being harder to cover <b>strengthens</b> the argument.',
          'E': 'Even more leave makes the problem bigger: this strengthens it.'},
     gap='Find the reason the proposals are bad for business (covering absent staff) and look for the option that fixes it.'),

dict(n=7, orig=11, answer='E', type='Parallel reasoning',
     passage=[('prem', 'As long as there is the right combination of guests, any party will be a success.'),
              ('evid', 'The last party was great,'),
              ('conc', 'so there must have been a perfect selection of guests.')],
     stem='Which one of the following most closely parallels the reasoning used in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'As long as there is a high level of unemployment, all economies will perform below their maximum potential. Unemployment levels here have risen sharply, so our economy will perform less well than it could do.',
              'B': 'As long as there are good hotels, any seaside town can get on the tourist trail. Our town is a tourist hotspot, yet none of the hotels are any good.',
              'C': 'As long as there is somebody in charge, any project will go smoothly. James has taken firm control, so this week’s project will be fine.',
              'D': 'As long as there are lots of security personnel around, public order is sure to be maintained. We have been having huge problems with public disorder here, so there cannot be many security people.',
              'E': 'As long as there is lots of water, any habitat will support life. Life is abundant in that habitat, so there must be lots of water.'},
     right='Same shape and same mistake: if A then B; B happened; so A must be true. Something else could have caused B.',
     why={'A': 'If A then B; A happened; so B. Goes forwards from the condition.',
          'B': 'States an exception (B without A) and draws no “must” conclusion.',
          'C': 'If A then B; A happened (James in charge); so B. Goes forwards.',
          'D': 'If A then B; B did <b>not</b> happen; so not A. A different (and valid) shape.'},
     working_title='Working: strip it to a skeleton',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Original: if <b>A</b> (right guests) then <b>B</b> (success) {ar('pen')} B happened (great party) {ar('pen')} so A <b>must</b> have been true.</p>
<p>The flaw: A is enough for B, but B might have had another cause (great food, great music).</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>E</b><span>If A (lots of water) then B (life) → B (abundant life) → so A must be true. {tick()} Same shape, same flaw.</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>If A (unemployment) then B → A happened → so B. Forwards. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>B</b><span>If A (good hotels) then B (tourists) → B but not A. Just an exception. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>If A (someone in charge) then B → A (James) → so B. Forwards. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>If A (security) then B (order) → not B → so not A. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('All five start with “As long as…”, so the first sentence can’t separate them. Look at what the second sentence gives you and what is concluded.', 'tip')}"""),

