dict(n=10, orig=17, answer='B', type='Principle',
     passage=[('ctx', 'There has been a choral singing boom.'),
              ('evid', 'Three hundred thousand more people sing in choirs than play amateur football, yet football receives £30 million from the government every year compared to £1 million for choirs.'),
              ('conc', 'It is only fair that a substantial increase in funding is made that reflects the numbers of participants and thus treats choral singers fairly.'),
              ('prem', 'The benefits for those who sing in a choir are comparable to those who play football: increased lung capacity, better posture, improvements to the immune system, and a general sense of well-being. A choir also fosters teamwork, where there is cooperation, determination, discipline and imagination, drawing the best from every individual involved and creating something much greater than the sum of its parts.')],
     stem='Which one of the following illustrates the principle used in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'The better-off countries have to tackle poverty in our world when we still have over a billion people living on less than a dollar a day.',
              'B': 'Audience figures at regional theatres are now greater than in the capital, so they deserve an increased share of the financial aid set aside for the arts.',
              'C': 'The proportion of people needing treatment for mental health issues has increased by 25% in a decade and more funding is needed to deal with this crisis.',
              'D': 'If citizens are able to raise a large amount of money towards a community sports centre, then the local council should show good faith by matching that amount.',
              'E': 'Our arts sector continues to generate an increasing share of national income, so we need to keep building on this success by encouraging the next generation of young actors, musicians, and other artists.'},
     right='Principle: public money should be shared out in line with how many people take part. Bigger regional audiences, so a bigger share of arts aid.',
     why={'A': 'About a duty to help those in need, not sharing funding by numbers.',
          'C': 'More need means more money, but there is no comparison with another group’s share.',
          'D': 'About matching money that people raise themselves.',
          'E': 'About building on economic success, not numbers taking part.'},
     working_title='Working: write the principle first',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Choirs have <b>more participants</b> than football {ar('pen')} but get far <b>less money</b> {ar('pen')} unfair: funding should reflect numbers.<br>
So the principle: <b>when one group has more participants than another, it deserves a bigger share of the funding</b>.</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>B</b><span>Regional theatres now have bigger audiences than the capital → they deserve a bigger share of arts aid. {tick()} Same rule, new setting.</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>Rich countries must tackle poverty: a duty based on need. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>C</b><span>Need has gone up → more funding. No comparison between two groups. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>Match what citizens raise: rewards effort, not numbers. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>Arts earn money → invest in young artists: about economic success. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('The key words are “reflects the numbers of participants”. Look for the option where a share of money follows the number of people.', 'tip')}"""),

dict(n=11, orig=21, answer='C', type='Parallel reasoning',
     passage=[('prem', 'In real terms, spending on education in the UK is going down.'),
              ('prem', 'But reductions in funding always mean a reduction in the quality of education provided.'),
              ('conc', 'In the current climate, we should expect to see the quality of education in the UK diminishing.')],
     stem='Which one of the following most closely parallels the reasoning used in the above passage?',
     options={'A': 'No one ever succeeds at anything unless they make an effort. But you’ve just won an award for your work in physics, so you must have made an effort.',
              'B': 'When the water is at this level, it’s either high tide or a flood. High tide is not for another eight hours. So there must be a flood upstream.',
              'C': 'If you take regular exercise, you’ll be fit in heart and mind. You’ve been working out twice a week for three months now, so you must be happy and healthy.',
              'D': 'If you eat sweets every day you’re more likely to need dental treatment. Sam has had a lot of dental treatment so he must eat sweets every day.',
              'E': 'The car won’t run without a working battery. I can’t get the car to start so the battery must be dead.'},
     right='Same shape: A always leads to B; A is happening; so B. Regular exercise → fit; you exercise; so you are fit.',
     why={'A': 'Starts from the result (success) and works back to what it needs (effort).',
          'B': 'An either/or: one option ruled out, so the other.',
          'D': 'Works back from the result (dental treatment) to the cause, and it is only “more likely”.',
          'E': 'Works back from the result (won’t start) to one possible cause.'},
     working_title='Working: strip it to a skeleton',
     working=f"""<p class="skel">Original: <b>A</b> is happening (spending down) + A <b>always</b> leads to <b>B</b> (lower quality) {ar('pen')} so expect B.</p>
<p>It goes <b>forwards</b> from the cause to the result. The order of the sentences doesn’t matter.</p>
<div class="map">
<div><b>C</b><span>A (regular exercise) → B (fit in heart and mind) + you do A (twice a week) → so B (happy and healthy). {tick()} Same shape.</span></div>
<div><b>A</b><span>B (success) needs A (effort) + B happened → so A. Backwards. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>B</b><span>Either X (high tide) or Y (flood) + not X → so Y. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>D</b><span>A (sweets) → more likely B (dentist) + B → so A. Backwards. {cross()}</span></div>
<div><b>E</b><span>No A (battery) → no B (car runs) + no B → so no A. Backwards. {cross()}</span></div>
</div>
{note('Mark each option “forwards” (cause given, result concluded) or “backwards” (result given, cause concluded).', 'tip')}"""),

dict(n=12, orig=22, answer='A', type='Flaw',
     passage=[('ctx', 'Maths is the most popular subject taken at A Level in the UK. Most of the best-paid jobs are in the technology and finance sectors, which tend to require maths. The majority of students achieving the best grades in maths are boys, which helps to explain the persistence of the gender pay disparity. But what explains the gender disparity in Maths A Level results – genetic inheritance or social conditioning?'),
              ('evid', 'International studies of achievement in maths show that in some countries girls outperform boys in maths,'),
              ('ic', 'suggesting that social conditioning is the explanation.'),
              ('conc', 'From this we can conclude that the better achievement in maths of boys in the UK is caused by low expectations of girls in maths by teachers and parents.')],
     stem='Which one of the following best expresses the flaw in the above argument?',
     options={'A': 'It assumes that the only factors in social conditioning are the expectations of teachers and parents.',
              'B': 'It fails to explain why the relative achievement of boys and girls varies country by country.',
              'C': 'It fails to consider the changing picture of achievement by boys and girls over time.',
              'D': 'It ignores the relatively better achievement of girls in some other subjects.',
              'E': 'It confuses the gender pay disparity with achievement disparities.'},
     right='The evidence points to “social conditioning” in general. The conclusion narrows it to teachers’ and parents’ expectations, ignoring friends, media and so on.',
     why={'B': 'It does explain it: social conditioning differs between countries.',
          'C': 'Changes over time aren’t needed to get from social conditioning to the conclusion.',
          'D': 'Other subjects don’t affect the reasoning about maths.',
          'E': 'It treats them separately: grades help explain pay, but it doesn’t mix them up.'},
     gap='Compare the intermediate conclusion (social conditioning) with the main conclusion (teachers and parents). It has narrowed.'),

